WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.500 --> 00:00:12.020
CI/CD is the system that transforms source code, dependencies, build scripts, compilers, package managers, tests, secrets, and configuration into release artifacts.

2
00:00:12.120 --> 00:00:27.640
For C++ projects, that system may compile native code, link libraries, generate code, package binaries, create installers, run sanitizers, produce debug symbols, sign artifacts, publish packages, and trigger deployments.

3
00:00:27.740 --> 00:00:32.060
It is where many separate engineering decisions become one shipped result.

4
00:00:32.340 --> 00:00:34.660
That makes the pipeline more than automation.

5
00:00:34.760 --> 00:00:36.840
It is part of the product's security boundary.

6
00:00:36.940 --> 00:00:51.100
A team can write careful C++ and still lose release confidence if the build environment is inconsistent, the workflow is changed without review, the signing process is unclear, or the artifact cannot be traced back to approved inputs.

7
00:00:51.380 --> 00:00:54.980
Secure CI/CD makes important facts visible.

8
00:00:55.080 --> 00:00:57.720
Which source revision was built?

9
00:00:57.820 --> 00:01:01.900
Which dependency versions and toolchains shaped the binary?

10
00:01:02.000 --> 00:01:03.840
Which checks ran?

11
00:01:03.940 --> 00:01:06.020
Which job produced the artifact?

12
00:01:06.120 --> 00:01:07.880
Which approval allowed promotion?

13
00:01:07.980 --> 00:01:09.740
Which signing policy applied?

14
00:01:09.840 --> 00:01:16.960
When these facts are recorded, teams can review releases, respond to incidents, and explain decisions without guessing.

15
00:01:17.240 --> 00:01:20.280
The goal is not to make pipelines slow or ceremonial.

16
00:01:20.380 --> 00:01:22.540
The goal is trustworthy automation.

17
00:01:22.640 --> 00:01:33.920
Secure pipelines protect build inputs, isolate build environments, scope secrets, run useful checks, retain evidence, and promote known artifacts through controlled release steps.

18
00:01:34.020 --> 00:01:41.140
For native C++ software, that discipline is essential because the final binary reflects much more than application source code.

19
00:01:41.240 --> 00:01:48.200
It reflects the toolchain, build mode, linked components, packaging rules, and release approvals around that code.
